AIE2 machine code, read statically: VLIW bundles, the accumulator file, and the cost model
2026-09-09. env: mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201 Peano) on native Windows,
Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G). NO HARDWARE WAS USED. Every number below comes from
disassembling object code with Peano's own llvm-objdump, or from compiling with Peano's clang.
The NPU was not opened, no hardware context was taken, and this runs while the device is busy.

Commands (from the repo root; no ironenv activation needed):
  python tools/aie_disasm.py <object> --loops --slots --regs [--verbose]
  python kernels/acc_spill_probe/sweep.py --max 12

WHY: docs/SILICON.md 1.2 carries MACs per cycle, vector width and the mmul shapes as SPEC rows
copied out of AMD's device.yaml, never measured. VLIW slot count and issue width appear in no
document in this repo at all. docs/DECISIONS.md says "AIE2 has only 6 hardware accumulator
registers" while docs/SILICON.md 1.2 says "<=4 stays in registers ... TO VERIFY the exact
register count from the AIE-ML ISA rather than from that one kernel" -- two documents, two
different numbers, neither measured. S0 measured the clock, which makes cycles convertible to
seconds; this reads the cycles themselves off the machine code.

METHOD: llvm-objdump from the llvm-aie wheel disassembles elf32-aie and prints each VLIW
bundle's slots separated by ';'. tools/aie_disasm.py parses that, groups bundles into
hardware-loop bodies, and counts slot occupancy, allocated registers and stack references.
A loop body is taken as every bundle from the nearest preceding label through the bundle
labelled .L_LEnd*, which is the last bundle of a zero-overhead hardware loop.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE BUNDLE FORMAT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The nop mnemonics name the slots. A full-width bundle is 16 bytes and prints six fields:

    nopb ; nopa ; nops ; nopx ; nopm ; nopv

so the slots are b (branch), a (load), s (store), x (scalar), m (move), v (vector). When both
x and m are empty they print as one fused `nopxm` field, so a five-field bundle still occupies
six slots. Bundles that use few slots are emitted in a compressed encoding shorter than 16
bytes; they still issue in one cycle, so cycles are counted by bundle and never by byte.

Evidence, one bundle of each kind from the clock probe's own kernel:

    0x0170  nopb ; nopa ; nops ; nopxm ; nopv                             (5 fields, all idle)
    0x00c0  nopb ; nopa ; nops ; nopx ; vbcst.32 x0, r17 ; nopv           (6 fields, m busy)
    0x01b0  nopb ; nopa ; st r2,[p0,#0] ; add r17,r17,#0x1 ; nopm ; nopv  (6 fields, s and x)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. CALIBRATION: STATIC BUNDLE COUNT IS THE CYCLE COUNT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
results/aie/clock_probe_npu.log measured two loops on hardware at exactly 9.000 and 2.000
cycles per iteration, constant from 2^18 to 2^25 iterations. Those same two loops, read out of
the object the JIT compiled for that run:

