        0x0250  event	#0x1
        0x025c  event	#0x0
        0x0260  event	#0x1
    loop .L_LEnd0 0x2d0-0x300: 8 bundles = 8 cycles/iteration, 13 live ops, density 1.62 of 6
        0x02d0  st	r16, [p1, dj0] | lshl	r7, r6, r5
        0x02e0  mov	dj0, r7
        0x02e4  st	r16, [p1, dj0] | add	r8, r7, #0xc
        0x02ea  add	r9, r7, #0x8 | mov	dj0, r8
        0x02f2  mov	dj1, r9
        0x02f6  st	r16, [p1, dj0] | add	r10, r7, #0x4
        0x02fc  mov	dj0, r10
        0x0300  st	r16, [p1, dj1] | add	r6, r6, #0x4

The scalar loop is 9 bundles and measured 9.000 cycles. The vector loop is 2 bundles and
measured 2.000. Both exact.

That equality is not a coincidence and it generalises: AIE2 is a statically scheduled VLIW with
an exposed pipeline, so the compiler covers every operand latency with explicit nop bundles
instead of leaving it to a hardware interlock. The scalar loop shows the mechanism directly --
six consecutive all-nop bundles sit between the load at 0x0150 and the add at 0x01ac that
consumes it, so a scalar load's result is available to the seventh bundle after it issues.

CONSEQUENCE, and this is the useful part: the cycles per iteration of any hardware loop on this
part can be read off the disassembly before the kernel is ever run. A kernel's inner-loop cost
no longer needs a throughput fit.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. COMPILING FOR AIE2 WITHOUT IRON, AND THE CHECK THAT IT IS THE SAME COMPILE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Peano's clang compiles a kernel for the core directly:

  clang++ --target=aie2-none-unknown-elf -std=c++20 -O2 -D__AIE_API_AIE_ADF_HPP__=1 \
      -c -I <mlir_aie>/include kernel.cc -o kernel.o

The one non-obvious flag predefines the include guard of aie_api's graph-level ADF header so
its body is skipped. That header includes <adf.h>, which ships with Vitis and exists nowhere on
this machine (searched: the mlir-aie tree, the llvm-aie wheel, C:\Program Files\RyzenAI,
C:\Xilinx). Nothing in a Peano kernel uses the ADF graph API.

The flag is safe because recompiling the clock probe's own source this way reproduces the
object IRON built for the hardware run, structure for structure:

  IRON's cached object:
== C:\Users\<user>/.npu/cache\1a0df11de1bfd54080e1a5ef\clock_kernels.o
  section .text.clock_probe: 102 bundles (29 full-width, 73 compressed), frame 32 B, stack refs 0
    loop .L_LEnd3 0xc0-0xd0: 2 bundles = 2 cycles/iteration, 3 live ops, density 1.50 of 6
    loop .L_LEnd2 0x150-0x1b0: 9 bundles = 9 cycles/iteration, 4 live ops, density 0.44 of 6
    loop .L_LEnd1 0x200-0x260: 8 bundles = 8 cycles/iteration, 8 live ops, density 1.00 of 6
    loop .L_LEnd0 0x2d0-0x300: 8 bundles = 8 cycles/iteration, 13 live ops, density 1.62 of 6

  rebuilt standalone with the command above:
== C:\Users\<user>\.claude\jobs\8a20f3f5\tmp\clock_rebuilt.o
  section .text.clock_probe: 102 bundles (29 full-width, 73 compressed), frame 32 B, stack refs 0
    loop .L_LEnd3 0xc0-0xd0: 2 bundles = 2 cycles/iteration, 3 live ops, density 1.50 of 6
    loop .L_LEnd2 0x150-0x1b0: 9 bundles = 9 cycles/iteration, 4 live ops, density 0.44 of 6
    loop .L_LEnd1 0x200-0x260: 8 bundles = 8 cycles/iteration, 8 live ops, density 1.00 of 6
    loop .L_LEnd0 0x2d0-0x300: 8 bundles = 8 cycles/iteration, 13 live ops, density 1.62 of 6

Same bundle count, same split of full-width and compressed, same frame, same four loops at
the same addresses. Static analysis of AIE2 therefore needs no NPU, no hardware context and no
IRON, and can run against a busy device.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THE ACCUMULATOR REGISTER FILE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
kernels/acc_spill_probe/ holds K live aie::mmul<4,8,8,int8,int8,acc32> accumulators across a
k-reduction loop, the shape upstream's conv2dk3 uses, and sweeps K. Two signals: the distinct
accumulator registers the allocator names, and stack references anywhere in the function.

Live aie::mmul<4,8,8,int8,int8,acc32> accumulators vs register pressure
  source   C:\Users\<user>\PycharmProjects\ryzen-ai-xdna1-quantization\.claude\worktrees\curious-tinkering-whistle\kernels\acc_spill_probe\acc_kernels.cc
  clang    C:\Users\<user>/mlir-aie/ironenv/Lib/site-packages/llvm-aie/bin/clang++.exe
  objects  C:\Users\<user>\AppData\Local\Temp\acc_spill_yv_7sju9

live acc  acc regs  loop cycles  cycles/MAC  stack refs  frame B  bundles
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
       1         1            -         nan           0        -       42
       2         2            8        4.00           0        -       38
       3         4           14        4.67           0        -       44
       4         6           15        3.75           0        -       47
       5         6           13        2.60           0        -       50
       6         9           15        2.50           5        -       61
       7         9           24        3.43          17       96       81
       8         9           27        3.38          25        -      100
       9         9           28        3.11          32      256      105
      10         9           24        2.40          48        -       95
      11         9           29        2.64          72        -      114
      12         9           36        3.00          96        -      133

Highest count with zero stack traffic: 5
First count with any stack traffic:    6
Most accumulator registers allocated:  9 at 6 live accumulators (cm0 cm1 cm2 cm3 cm4 cm5 cm6 cm7 cm8)
Cheapest cycles per MAC without spill: 2.60 at 5 accumulators

READING: the allocator names nine accumulator registers, cm0 through cm8. It reaches nine at
six live accumulators and never goes past it however many more are asked for. Five live
accumulators of this shape compile with zero stack traffic; six is the first count that touches
the stack at all.

Both existing claims in the docs are wrong, in opposite directions. docs/DECISIONS.md's "only 6
hardware accumulator registers" is below the nine register names that actually appear.
docs/SILICON.md's "<=4 stays in registers" is one below the real spill-free ceiling of five.
The conv2dk3 width fix in kernels/conv2dk3_widthfix/ was written to N<=4 for safety and is
therefore correct but conservative; five is available.

CAVEAT: this is the spill-free ceiling for ONE accumulator shape, 4x8x8 int8 with an int32
accumulator, under -O2. A wider accumulator would fit fewer. The nine register names are a
lower bound on the architectural file, since the allocator also needs accumulators for
temporaries and may simply never have needed a tenth.


