READING: the inner loop is nine bundles and issues eight vmac instructions, one per live
accumulator cm0..cm7. On AIE2 one int8 vmac is the 256-MAC operation the 256 MACs/cycle
nameplate describes, so the loop issues 8/9 = 0.889 vector MACs per cycle, 88.9 percent of the
machine's MAC issue rate. Its operands arrive on the a and s slots in the same bundles, four
vlda and four vldb per iteration, so the loads are free.

That number sits against the measured whole-kernel rate. 4607.05 GOPS over 16 cores at the
measured 1.7983 GHz is 31.3 percent of the 14,730 GOPS those cores can issue. The inner loop is
at 88.9 percent. The missing factor is therefore NOT the inner loop's instruction schedule, and
rewriting it is not where the time is; it is in how much of the elapsed time is spent inside
that loop at all.

The function does spill -- 416 byte frame, 37 stack references, consistent with section 4 since
it holds eight live accumulators where five is the spill-free ceiling -- but none of that
traffic is in the nine loop bundles above, so the spills cost setup time per call and not
per-iteration throughput.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Nothing here is a hardware measurement. It is what the compiler emitted. The bundle-equals-
  cycle identity is checked against two measured loops and no more; a loop that waits on a
  lock, a stream or a DMA takes longer than its bundle count and the disassembly cannot say by
  how much. That is what the trace unit's stall events are for and it is the next objective.
- The 88.9 percent figure is the inner loop's issue density, not the kernel's utilisation.
- One accumulator shape, one optimisation level, one compiler version.
- Slot names come from the nop mnemonics llvm-objdump prints. They are not from a published
  AIE-ML ISA document, which this project does not have.
